/**
 * @file modules.dox
 * @brief Doxygen module (group) definitions for the Uge engine.
 *
 * This file only declares the documentation groups and their descriptions. Types are
 * attached to a group with @c \@ingroup in the corresponding header.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup group_core Core
 * @brief Application lifecycle, layers, windowing, logging and fundamental utilities.
 *
 * Uge::EntryPoint provides `main`, which constructs the client-supplied
 * Uge::Application. The application owns a Uge::Window, an ImGui overlay and a
 * Uge::LayerStack, and drives the frame loop: poll input, update every layer with a
 * Uge::Timestep, render ImGui, swap buffers.
 *
 * This group also holds the pieces every other module depends on: Uge::Ref and
 * Uge::Scope smart-pointer aliases, the Uge::Log logging facade and the `UG_ASSERT` /
 * `UG_VERIFY` macro families, Uge::UUID identifiers, and the raw Uge::Buffer type.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup group_events Events
 * @brief Input and window events, and the dispatcher that routes them through layers.
 *
 * The platform window translates OS callbacks into Uge::Event subclasses. The
 * application hands each event to the layer stack from top to bottom; a layer uses
 * Uge::EventDispatcher to match the event type it cares about and sets
 * Uge::Event::Handled to stop propagation.
 *
 * Events are grouped by Uge::EventCategory so a layer can filter broadly (for example,
 * "all input events") without testing each concrete type.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup group_renderer Renderer
 * @brief The graphics-API abstraction and the engine's three independent draw paths.
 *
 * Every renderer resource is a platform-agnostic interface with a static `Create`
 * factory that switches on Uge::RendererAPI::GetAPI() and returns the concrete backend
 * implementation from @ref group_platform. Adding a rendering feature therefore means
 * touching both the interface here and the OpenGL class that implements it.
 *
 * @warning The three draw paths do **not** share state. Each maintains its own shaders,
 * uniform buffers and batching, and each needs its own begin/end pair:
 * - Uge::Renderer2D — batched sprites, circles, lines and MSDF text; writes a per-vertex
 *   entity ID used for mouse picking in the editor.
 * - Uge::Renderer3D — standalone textured-cube primitives.
 * - Uge::Model — the mesh path used for imported 3D models, with its own
 *   Uge::Model::BeginScene and Uge::Model::EndScene.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup group_scene Scene
 * @brief The EnTT-backed scene graph, entities, components and scene serialization.
 *
 * Uge::Scene wraps an `entt::registry` and is itself an Uge::Asset. Uge::Entity is a thin
 * handle over an `entt::entity` plus its owning scene, so entities are cheap to copy and
 * pass by value.
 *
 * Every entity created through Uge::Scene::CreateEntity receives an Uge::IDComponent
 * (a stable Uge::UUID), a Uge::TagComponent (its display name) and a
 * Uge::TransformComponent.
 *
 * @par Adding a new component type
 * A new component needs five coordinated changes:
 * 1. the struct itself in `Components.h`,
 * 2. an entry in Uge::ComponentGroup so copy/duplicate covers it,
 * 3. a Uge::Scene::OnComponentAdded template specialization,
 * 4. read and write support in Uge::SceneSerializer,
 * 5. an editing UI in Uge::SceneHierarchyPanel.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup group_asset Assets
 * @brief Asset handles, the editor and runtime asset managers, and the importers.
 *
 * An Uge::AssetHandle is a Uge::UUID. Uge::Asset is the polymorphic base shared by
 * Uge::Scene, Uge::Texture2D, Uge::Model and Uge::Material; each subclass supplies
 * `GetStaticType()` and `GetType()`.
 *
 * Uge::AssetManager is a static facade that forwards to the manager owned by the active
 * Uge::Project, so anything asset-related requires a loaded project. Two implementations
 * of Uge::AssetManagerBase exist: Uge::EditorAssetManager, which is registry-backed and
 * imports from disk on demand, and Uge::RuntimeAssetManager, which serves pre-packed
 * assets.
 *
 * Uge::AssetImporter::ImportAsset dispatches on Uge::AssetMetadata::Type to
 * Uge::TextureImporter, Uge::MeshImporter or Uge::SceneImporter.
 *
 * @note Assets with no backing file — notably the Uge::Material objects synthesized
 * during mesh import — are registered with Uge::EditorAssetManager::AddMemoryOnlyAsset
 * and are deliberately not written to the on-disk registry.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup group_project Project
 * @brief The active project, its configuration and its `.ugproj` serialization.
 *
 * Uge::Project is a global singleton backed by a YAML `.ugproj` file read and written by
 * Uge::ProjectSerializer. It owns the asset manager and defines where content lives.
 *
 * All content paths are relative to Uge::Project::GetAssetDirectory(). Use the
 * `Project::GetAsset*Path*` helpers rather than composing paths by hand, so that
 * relative and absolute forms stay consistent.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup group_scripting Scripting
 * @brief The embedded Mono runtime and the C++/C# internal-call bridge.
 *
 * Uge::ScriptEngine embeds Mono, loads the assembly named by
 * Uge::ProjectConfig::ScriptModulePath, and instantiates a managed object for every
 * entity carrying a Uge::ScriptComponent. Uge::ScriptGlue registers the native functions
 * that back the `InternalCalls` class in the `Uge-ScriptCore` C# library.
 *
 * @note Adding an internal call requires matching changes on both sides: the native
 * registration in `ScriptGlue.cpp` and the `[MethodImplAttribute(InternalCall)]`
 * declaration in `InternalCalls.cs`. A mismatch fails at runtime, not at compile time.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup group_platform Platform
 * @brief Backend implementations of the platform-agnostic interfaces.
 *
 * Everything here is selected at runtime by the `Create` factories in
 * @ref group_renderer and @ref group_core. Client code should never name these classes
 * directly.
 *
 * - `Platform/OpenGL` — the OpenGL 4.5 implementation of every renderer resource.
 * - `Platform/Windows` — the GLFW-backed window, the Win32 input polling and the native
 *   file dialogs.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup group_editor Editor
 * @brief The Uge-Editor application layer and its dockable panels.
 *
 * Uge::EditorLayer holds nearly all editor behaviour: the viewport and its framebuffer,
 * play/simulate/stop state transitions, gizmo manipulation, mouse picking and the
 * scene open/save commands. The panels under `UgeEditor/src/Panels` are owned by the
 * layer and render inside its ImGui dockspace.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup group_math Math
 * @brief Small math helpers layered on top of GLM.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup group_debug Debug
 * @brief Instrumentation and profiling support.
 *
 * The `UG_PROFILE_*` macros write Chrome-tracing JSON sessions that can be loaded in
 * `chrome://tracing` or https://ui.perfetto.dev.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup group_ui UI
 * @brief Reusable ImGui widget helpers shared by the editor panels.
 */
